            <fr:route>/MZIY/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The three units and the one data path">The three units and the one data path</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="anki">1786395976741</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p>What are the three units inside a TPU <fr:link href="/OGCRQ/" title="TensorCore (TPU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCRQ/" display-uri="OGCRQ" type="local">TensorCore (TPU)</fr:link>, and what is the data path from <fr:link href="/AI2G/" title="High bandwidth memory" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/AI2G/" display-uri="AI2G" type="local">HBM</fr:link> to compute?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  <html:ul><html:li><html:strong><fr:link href="/OGCRP/" title="Matrix Multiply Unit (MXU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCRP/" display-uri="OGCRP" type="local">MXU</fr:link></html:strong> — matrix multiply on a systolic array. Nearly all of the FLOPs.</html:li>
    <html:li><html:strong><fr:link href="/OGCRN/" title="Vector Processing Unit" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCRN/" display-uri="OGCRN" type="local">VPU</fr:link></html:strong> — elementwise ops (ReLU, add, mul) and reductions.</html:li>
    <html:li><html:strong><fr:link href="/GFK2/" title="VMEM" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/GFK2/" display-uri="GFK2" type="local">VMEM</fr:link></html:strong> — an on-chip scratchpad in the TensorCore, like L1/L2 but far larger (~128 MiB on v5e) and <html:em>programmer-controlled</html:em> rather than a cache.</html:li></html:ul>

  The path is HBM <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\to ]]></fr:tex> VMEM <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\to ]]></fr:tex> <fr:link href="/OGCRO/" title="Vector Register" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCRO/" display-uri="OGCRO" type="local">VREGs</fr:link> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\to ]]></fr:tex> MXU/VPU <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\to ]]></fr:tex> VMEM <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\to ]]></fr:tex> HBM. Nothing computes directly out of HBM. All of it is pipelined — the copies overlap with MXU work, so the array never stalls waiting on memory.
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors>
              <fr:author>
                <fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link>
              </fr:author>
            </fr:authors>
            <fr:date>
              <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
              <fr:month>5</fr:month>
              <fr:day>22</fr:day>
            </fr:date>
            <fr:uri>https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCRP/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>OGCRP</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/OGCRP/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Matrix Multiply Unit (MXU)">Matrix Multiply Unit (MXU)</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter><html:p>A core component of the <fr:link href="/NZZ7/" title="TPU Architecture" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/NZZ7/" display-uri="NZZ7" type="local">TPU architecture</fr:link> responsible for performing matrix multiplication via a weight stationary <fr:link href="/SXP6/" title="Systolic array" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/SXP6/" display-uri="SXP6" type="local">Systolic array</fr:link>.</html:p><html:ul><html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[128 \times  128]]></fr:tex> — 16,384 <html:em>multiply accumulate</html:em> units, each multiplying and adding in one cycle. v6e/Trillium widens the array to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[256 \times  256]]></fr:tex>, i.e. 65,536.</html:li>
  <html:li>The native op is one <html:code>bf16[8,128] @ bf16[128,128] -&gt; f32[8,128]</html:code> every 8 cycles, or about <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[5e13]]></fr:tex> bf16 FLOPs/s per MXU at 1.5 GHz. A v5e chip has enough MXUs to reach ~<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2e14]]></fr:tex>.</html:li></html:ul>
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(219, 216, 215);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(109, 103, 101);">Remark.</html:span> 
The side length is fixed in hardware, so every dimension has to be tiled down to it and any axis shorter than it is padded up to it, wasting the FLOPs spent on the padding. The order is
<html:ol><html:li>Tiling the matrix to load from <fr:link href="/AI2G/" title="High bandwidth memory" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/AI2G/" display-uri="AI2G" type="local">HBM</fr:link> to <fr:link href="/GFK2/" title="VMEM" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/GFK2/" display-uri="GFK2" type="local">VMEM</fr:link>.</html:li>
  <html:li>Compiler tiles from VMEM to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[128 \times  128]]></fr:tex> tiles to load into the MXU.</html:li></html:ol>
</html:div>
<fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>8</fr:month><fr:day>10</fr:day></fr:date><fr:uri>https://kellenkanarios.com/HPSO/</fr:uri><fr:display-uri>HPSO</fr:display-uri><fr:route>/HPSO/</fr:route><fr:title text="Why the MXU pads everything to 128">Why the MXU pads everything to 128</fr:title><fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon><fr:meta name="anki">1786395976465</fr:meta><fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p>What is the native op of one <fr:link href="/OGCRP/" title="Matrix Multiply Unit (MXU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCRP/" display-uri="OGCRP" type="local">Matrix Multiply Unit (MXU)</fr:link>, and why does that force padding to 128?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  One <html:code>bf16[8,128] @ bf16[128,128] -&gt; f32[8,128]</html:code> every 8 cycles, on a <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[128 \times  128]]></fr:tex> systolic array — <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[256 \times  256]]></fr:tex> on v6e/Trillium.

  The array's side length is fixed in hardware, so any matrix axis shorter than 128 (256 on v6e) is <html:strong>padded</html:strong> up to it, and the FLOPs spent on the padding are simply wasted. With 2–4 MXUs per core, tiling dimensions want to exceed <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[128 \times ]]></fr:tex> the MXU count.

  Scale: roughly <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[5e13]]></fr:tex> bf16 FLOPs/s per MXU at 1.5 GHz, so about <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2e14]]></fr:tex> per v5e chip. int8 runs ~<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2\times ]]></fr:tex> faster, int4 ~<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[4\times ]]></fr:tex>.
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree></fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors>
              <fr:author>
                <fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link>
              </fr:author>
            </fr:authors>
            <fr:date>
              <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
              <fr:month>7</fr:month>
              <fr:day>14</fr:day>
            </fr:date>
            <fr:uri>https://kellenkanarios.com/TJLA/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>TJLA</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/TJLA/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="KV-cache">KV-cache</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter><html:p>The keys and values of every token seen so far, held in memory across decoding steps so that each is projected once rather than once per step. <fr:link href="/004G/" title="Self-Attention" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/004G/" display-uri="004G" type="local">Attention</fr:link> at step <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[t]]></fr:tex> needs <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k_j]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_j]]></fr:tex> for every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[j \le  t]]></fr:tex>, and each of those is a function of token <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[j]]></fr:tex> and the weights alone — nothing about it depends on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[t]]></fr:tex>. So it is computable once and never again, and the only real question is whether you will spend memory to avoid recomputing it.</html:p><html:p>The cost of not spending it is quadratic, which is easy to miss because per step it looks merely linear: admitting token <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[t]]></fr:tex> means rebuilding <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[t+1]]></fr:tex> key–value pairs, so a sequence of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex> tokens costs <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\sum _{t=1}^{S} t = S(S+1)/2]]></fr:tex> projections rather than <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex>. The query is the asymmetry that makes the trade available at all — only the newest position needs one, so <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q_t]]></fr:tex> is a single row either way, and decoding is a matrix–vector product against a <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[K]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> that grow by one row a step.</html:p><html:div class="kv-fig" /><html:script src="/bafkrmiaub5secxo2sxo5iwiednjjl44l2tc6mwubpkxpi522gae6jmgsoq.js" defer="" />
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(219, 216, 215);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(109, 103, 101);">Notation.</html:span> Following the <fr:link href="/scaling-book/" title="How to scale your model" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/scaling-book/" display-uri="scaling-book" type="local">scaling book</fr:link>: <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex> is the KV sequence length, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[K]]></fr:tex> the number of key/value heads, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[H]]></fr:tex> the head dimension, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[L]]></fr:tex> the layers, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex> the batch. The clash between <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[K]]></fr:tex> as a matrix and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[K]]></fr:tex> as a head count is unfortunate and universal.</html:div>
<html:p>What the cache costs is memory, and the shape of that cost is the reason it governs inference planning. Each token contributes a key and a value in every layer, so a batch holds</html:p><fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[2 \cdot  B \cdot  S \cdot  L \cdot  K \cdot  H \cdot  \mathrm {bytes}]]></fr:tex><html:p>which is linear in the sequence length and — unlike the weights — <html:em>per sequence</html:em>, so it cannot be amortised over the batch. Context length and batch size are therefore drawn from the same <fr:link href="/AI2G/" title="High bandwidth memory" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/AI2G/" display-uri="AI2G" type="local">High bandwidth memory</fr:link> budget, and past a certain context the cache dominates residency outright, which is <fr:link href="/2BGU/" title="Processing in Memory for ML" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/2BGU/" display-uri="2BGU" type="local">Processing in Memory for ML</fr:link>'s framing of the memory wall.</html:p><html:p>It also decides what decoding is bound by. At step <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[t]]></fr:tex> the model streams the entire cache through the <fr:link href="/OGCRP/" title="Matrix Multiply Unit (MXU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCRP/" display-uri="OGCRP" type="local">Matrix Multiply Unit (MXU)</fr:link> to do <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[O(S)]]></fr:tex> multiply-accumulates against a single query row, so the <fr:link href="/YBQD/" title="Arithmetic intensity" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/YBQD/" display-uri="YBQD" type="local">Arithmetic intensity</fr:link> is roughly one FLOP per byte read — far below the ridge point of any accelerator, and flat in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex>. Decoding is bandwidth-bound, and the cache is what it is bound by.</html:p><html:p>Every fix for that attacks a factor in the expression above rather than the idea of caching. <fr:link href="/W4YP/" title="POTW: Fast Transformer Decoding: One Write-Head is All You Need" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/W4YP/" display-uri="W4YP" type="local">MQA</fr:link> sets <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[K = 1]]></fr:tex> and shares one key/value head across all query heads; <fr:link href="/XUVV/" title="Group Query Attention" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/XUVV/" display-uri="XUVV" type="local">Group Query Attention</fr:link> sets it to a group count and interpolates; <fr:link href="/WM3M/" title="Sliding Window Attention" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/WM3M/" display-uri="WM3M" type="local">Sliding Window Attention</fr:link> bounds <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex> instead by refusing to attend past a window. The FLOPs are unchanged in all three — what changes is how many bytes have to move to supply them.</html:p><fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>8</fr:month><fr:day>18</fr:day></fr:date><fr:uri>https://kellenkanarios.com/J65M/</fr:uri><fr:display-uri>J65M</fr:display-uri><fr:route>/J65M/</fr:route><fr:title text="The KV cache scales with K, and is per-sequence">The KV cache scales with K, and is per-sequence</fr:title><fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon><fr:meta name="anki">1787063264798</fr:meta><fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p>How large is a <fr:link href="/TJLA/" title="KV-cache" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/TJLA/" display-uri="TJLA" type="local">KV-cache</fr:link>, and which dimension actually drives it?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  The cache has shape <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[2, S, L, K, H]]]></fr:tex> per sequence — the leading 2 is keys and values — so in int8 it is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2SLKH]]></fr:tex> bytes.

  <html:strong>Worked:</html:strong> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S = 8\mathrm {k}]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[L = 64]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[KH = D = 8192]]></fr:tex> gives <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2 \cdot  8192 \cdot  64 \cdot  8192 \approx  8]]></fr:tex> GiB for a <html:em>single</html:em> sequence.

  <html:strong>Two things in that expression decide inference planning.</html:strong> It scales with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[K]]></fr:tex>, the number of key/value heads, and not with the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N]]></fr:tex> query heads — which is the whole argument for <fr:link href="/XUVV/" title="Group Query Attention" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/XUVV/" display-uri="XUVV" type="local">Group Query Attention</fr:link> and <fr:link href="/W4YP/" title="POTW: Fast Transformer Decoding: One Write-Head is All You Need" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/W4YP/" display-uri="W4YP" type="local">MQA</fr:link> at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[K \ll  N]]></fr:tex>. And it is per sequence, so unlike the weights it cannot be amortised over the batch: it competes with batch size for the same <fr:link href="/AI2G/" title="High bandwidth memory" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/AI2G/" display-uri="AI2G" type="local">High bandwidth memory</fr:link> budget, and generation is bandwidth-bound on streaming it.
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree></fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
